  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThis morning's RAP analysis shows the Canadian high continuing to \nstrengthen: the surface feature now sits at 1034 mb squarely over \nthe Province of Quebec. The trailing ridge which extends over the \nUpper Great Lakes has the UP under a pronounced pressure gradient \nfrom 1029-1031 mb on the back end of the high. Gradient flow has \nveered southeasterly and surface winds are calm across the UP. With \nGOES water vapor imagery displaying a substantial dearth of \ntropospheric moisture and no clouds, tonight's waxing gibbous is \nclearly visible on this Autumnal Equinox.\n\nAs expected with clear sky radiational cooling, temperature trends \nso far show interior locations dropping into the low 30s. A Frost \nAdvisory is in effect through 9 AM for most counties of the UP for \ntemperatures reaching into the low to mid 30s before sunrise. \nDespite dry air aloft, saturation should be realized at the surface \nat interior locations, leading to frost formation. Today, increasing \nWAA raises daytime temperatures into the mid to high 60s and \novernight temperatures into the high 30s so that the NBM registers \nminimal chances of temperatures less than 36F (proxy for frost \nformation) through Wednesday night, despite clear skies. Thursday, \nas the influence of high pressure wanes slightly through the day, a \nshot of moisture from the Plains is able to reach northward to \nCanada before collapsing eastward toward the Upper Great Lakes. \nWhile this doesn't generate meaningful QPF within the CWA, it does \ncause Thursday night to cloud up across much of the UP. This and \nsoutherly WAA contribute to well-above freezing temps in the high \n30s to 40s Thursday night, as well as 20-40% chances of fog in \ninland areas. Thus, we appear to be past the frost threat - for now \n- after this morning.\n\nBy Friday, high temperatures reach 5-10 degrees above normal to be \nvery near 70F. Despite this slight anomaly, the EFI ESAT table \nthrough next Wednesday is nearly devoid of color, indicating\nECMWF forecasts very near the average of historical forecast \ndistributions for a five-week period centered on the forecast \ndate. In other words, near climate-normal conditions can be \nexpected for those variables analyzed by the EFI table (CAPE, \nCAPE-shear, wind speed and gusts, minT, maxT, QPF, and snow). \nThe cause of these near-normal parameters locally, however, is \nnot normal. The Canadian high which is driving UP conditions \nthis week is quite robust: LREF 500 mb heights shows the mid-\nlevel high reaching 100% of climatology for much of Wednesday as\nit tracks east toward James Bay. Split flow continues through \nSaturday as Canadian high pressure is mostly maintained, and the\nconjoining mid-level low deepens off the mid- Atlantic \ncoastline. Saturday evening, a second shot of moisture rides the\nwindward edge of a ridge centered over the Great Lakes north \ntoward Saskatchewan. Saturday night into Sunday still looks to \nbe the next best potential for precipitation, with the highest \nchances concentrated over the western UP. Sometime Monday, high \npressure at last appears to break down over eastern Canada as a \ntrough approaches from the North Pacific.",
